@endnode


@node VERSIONHISTORY "Version history"
@{b}Version history (0.4.0 -> 0.7.0)@{ub}

Grouped by minor version, since individual patch-level builds (the
third number) are not separately itemized here.

@{b}0.4.x@{ub}
  - Scheduling introduced for Backup and Sync Jobs (Daily/Interval at
    first, later extended - see 0.5.x).
  - Duplicate job names and running more than one job at once are both
    refused outright, rather than silently allowed.
  - Per-Backup-Job archive retention (keep last N, delete the rest).
  - Double-clicking a job opens it for editing.
  - A "No repository yet" notice when trying to create a job before any
    Repository exists.
  - A one-time disclaimer on a Sync Job's first save.

@{b}0.5.x@{ub}
  - Weekly and Monthly added to the Schedule options (alongside Daily/
    Interval).
  - SMB Share and SFTP (SSH) Repositories introduced, backed by the
    third-party smb2fs/ssh2fs handlers.
  - Repository list gained a File System column.
  - A status line under the progress gauge showing the exact result of
    the last run, and "[job] running..." while one is active.
  - Fixed: gauge text garbling after resizing the main window.
  - Stopping a backup mid-run now deletes the incomplete archive and
    logs the cancellation, instead of leaving a partial file behind.
  - "Remove History" added (History menu), distinct from "Remove All
    Logs".
  - BackupVaultWebDaemon introduced as a first, experimental prototype.
  - Fixed a system requester ("Please insert volume...") that could
    appear while mounting an SMB/SFTP share.
  - SMB/SFTP Mountlist files moved to DEVS:DOSDrivers/ and left in
    place after a successful mount, so the share also survives a
    reboot; removed again only when the Repository is deleted from
    within BackupVault, which now also unmounts it.
  - Deleting a Repository still used by a Backup Job is refused,
    listing which job(s) need to be repointed first.
  - Repository list shows "smb2-handler"/"ssh2-handler" plus that
    handler's own version for SMB/SFTP, instead of a blank/guessed
    filesystem type.

@{b}0.6.x@{ub}
  - Credential Manager introduced - SMB/SFTP Repositories now pick a
    saved username/password/note from a Cycle button instead of typing
    them in per Repository; the old "Test Connection" buttons were
    removed since Save already tests the same way.
  - Double-clicking a Repository or Credential opens it for editing,
    matching Backup Jobs.
  - Fixed: a file that had already been listed but disappeared before
    being read (e.g. deleted while a backup was mid-run) could corrupt
    every archive entry after it, not just fail that one file.
  - Fixed: a Backup Job whose source included BackupVault's own log
    directory would always fail on its own, still-open log file for
    that run; it's now skipped cleanly instead.
  - SMB/SFTP Repositories gained an optional "Path in share" field
    (with its own Select... browser) to target a subdirectory of the
    share instead of always its root.
  - All saved-state files (catalog, Repositories, Credentials, Jobs,
    Sync Jobs, Sync Job baselines) moved from directly under PROGDIR:
    into PROGDIR:data/ - an existing install's files are migrated
    there automatically, once.
  - A Backup Job can now optionally scan each file for viruses
    (xvs.library) before archiving it, with the result folded right
    into that file's own log line, e.g. "(scanned : passed)" - see
    @{" Creating a Backup Job " LINK CREATEJOB} and
    @{" Requirements " LINK REQUIREMENTS}.
  - "View Log" now opens a log in its own in-app window (one line per
    row, warnings/failures shown in bold) instead of always launching
    Multiview - an "Open in Multiview" button in that window still
    does, for whoever wants it. See @{" The History tab " LINK HISTORYTAB}.

@{b}0.7.x@{ub}
  - Per-Backup-Job Exclude patterns added (semicolon-separated AmigaDOS
    wildcards) - see @{" Creating a Backup Job " LINK CREATEJOB}.
  - A free-space warning before a manually-started Backup Job, if the
    destination doesn't look big enough for the source - see
    @{" Creating a Backup Job " LINK CREATEJOB}.
  - Sync Jobs gained a Preview option (Continue/Preview/Cancel) - see
    what a run would copy/delete/conflict without touching anything -
    see @{" Sync Jobs " LINK SYNCJOBS}.
  - Fixed: a one-way Sync Job could permanently stop re-copying a file
    to a destination that doesn't persist between runs (e.g. a RAM disk
    cleared on reboot), since it only checked whether the source had
    changed, never whether the destination still actually had the file
    - see @{" Sync Jobs " LINK SYNCJOBS}.
  - Fixed: a Sync Job that could not copy a specific file at all (most
    commonly a destination filesystem with a shorter maximum filename
    than the source) used to fail silently with no visible message; it
    is now logged plainly ("FAILED to copy ...") with a count in the
    run's summary - see @{" Sync Jobs " LINK SYNCJOBS}.
  - Fixed: a one-way Sync Job's progress bar could show roughly double
    the real number of files once its baseline was fully populated.
  - Repository list gained a Max Filename column for Local Repositories,
    found by actually testing the volume rather than trusting its
    reported filesystem type - see @{" Repositories " LINK REPOSITORIES}.
  - Repository list's File System column now identifies a Local
    Repository by asking its actual handler task, not just its reported
    DOS type - a volume can report a plain FFS-style type while a
    different handler (most commonly PFS3) really manages it, which the
    old dostype-only check could not detect - see
    @{" Repositories " LINK REPOSITORIES}.
  - BackupVault.jobextras changed from a fixed-size binary record to
    plain text internally, so it can keep gaining fields (like Exclude
    patterns, this release) without any risk of corrupting an existing
    install's file - migrated automatically, once, the first time you
    run this version.

Go back to the @{" main menu " LINK MAIN}.
@endnode


@node REQUIREMENTS "Requirements"
@{b}Requirements@{ub}

  - AmigaOS 3.x (3.0 or later recommended).
  - TabGroup.mcc and NList.mcc/NListview.mcc/NListtree.mcc - third-party
    MUI custom classes BackupVault's GUI is built on. Not part of
    AmigaOS - install them as MUI classes if you don't already have
    them (both are common, widely-used MCCs).
  - Optional: z.library (salass00/z_lib) for Deflate compression. If it
    is not installed, Deflate jobs simply store their files
    uncompressed instead.
  - Optional: smb2fs and/or ssh2fs (each a separate third-party
    package, not included) plus a working TCP/IP stack, only if you
    want to use an SMB Share or SFTP Repository.
  - Optional: xvs.library (Georg Wittmann / Virus Help Team, not
    included) for a Backup Job's "Scan for viruses before backup"
    option. If it is not installed, that option is simply skipped.

Go back to the @{" main menu " LINK MAIN}.
@endnode


@node INSTALL "Installation"
@{b}Installation@{ub}

  1. Copy the BackupVault drawer to your Amiga (or a partition of your
     choice).
  2. Make sure TabGroup.mcc and NList.mcc/NListview.mcc are installed
     (see @{" Requirements " LINK REQUIREMENTS}).
  3. Run "BackupVault" from Workbench or a Shell. The command-line tool
     "Backup" can be run directly from a Shell - see
     @{" The command-line tool " LINK CLITOOL}.

Go back to the @{" main menu " LINK MAIN}.
@endnode


@node PROGRAMS "Programs in this package"
@{b}Programs in this package@{ub}

@{b}BackupVault@{ub} - the MUI graphical front end this guide mostly
describes. The main program most people will use.

@{b}Backup@{ub} - a command-line tool for scripted use, sharing the same
archive format and doing the actual work behind every BackupVault GUI
operation. See @{" The command-line tool " LINK CLITOOL}.

@{b}BackupVaultDaemon@{ub} - @{b}EXPERIMENTAL, STILL IN DEVELOPMENT.@{ub} A
tiny headless CLI tool (no MUI, no window) that runs your saved,
scheduled Backup/Sync Jobs without BackupVault's own window open - an
alternative to leaving BackupVault itself running/iconified just for
scheduling. See @{" Headless daemon " LINK DAEMON}.

@{b}BackupVaultWebDaemon@{ub} - @{b}EXPERIMENTAL, STILL IN DEVELOPMENT, NOT
YET INTEGRATED INTO THE GUI.@{ub} A separate headless HTTP server that
lets you browse and download the contents of your Repositories from an
ordinary web browser. See @{" Web daemon " LINK WEBDAEMON}.

Both BackupVaultDaemon and BackupVaultWebDaemon may change
significantly, including their command-line options, before they are
considered stable - see @{" Known limitations " LINK LIMITATIONS} and
@{" Disclaimer " LINK DISCLAIMER}.

Go back to the @{" main menu " LINK MAIN}.
@endnode


@node BACKUPTAB "The Backup tab"
@{b}The Backup tab@{ub}

The Backup tab lists your saved Backup Jobs and Sync Jobs together, in
one list. Buttons:

  @{b}Create Backup@{ub}  - opens the Create Job dialog (Backup Job or Sync
                 Job, chosen by its own Type cycle) - see
                 @{" Creating a Backup Job " LINK CREATEJOB} and
                 @{" Sync Jobs " LINK SYNCJOBS}.
  @{b}Repository@{ub}     - opens the Repository manager - see
                 @{" Repositories " LINK REPOSITORIES}.
  @{b}Edit Job@{ub}       - opens the Create Job dialog, pre-filled, for
                 whichever job is selected in the list. A job's kind
                 (Backup or Sync) cannot be changed while editing -
                 delete and recreate it instead if you need to.
  @{b}Delete Job@{ub}     - removes the selected job (after confirming).
                 For a Sync Job this also removes its baseline file -
                 see @{" Files this program uses " LINK FILESCREATED}.

The Start button at the bottom of the window runs whichever job is
selected in the list - see @{" Running a job " LINK RUNNINGJOBS}.

Go back to the @{" main menu " LINK MAIN}.
@endnode


@node REPOSITORIES "Repositories"
@{b}Repositories@{ub}

A Repository is a named backup destination. Every Backup Job writes
into a Repository, chosen by name; more than one job can share the
same Repository. Three kinds, chosen by the "Type" cycle in the
Create/Edit Repository dialog:

  @{b}Local@{ub}     - a plain directory, same as always.
  @{b}SMB Share@{ub}  - a Windows/Samba network share, mounted via the
             third-party smb2fs handler (L:smb2-handler -
             https://github.com/salass00/smb2fs, not included with
             this program - must already be installed, along with a
             TCP/IP stack).
  @{b}SFTP (SSH)@{ub} - a share on an SSH server, mounted via the
             third-party ssh2fs handler (L:ssh2-handler - see the
             ssh2fs/ folder in this project's own source, or wherever
             you obtained it - also not included).

For SMB/SFTP, fill in the connection fields (Server/Share for SMB,
Host/Port for SFTP), pick a Credential for the username/password (see
@{" Credentials " LINK CREDENTIALS} - the dialog's own "Add Credential"
button gets you there without losing what you've typed if you don't
have one yet), and optionally fill in "Path in share" if you want the
Repository to point at a subdirectory of the share rather than its root
- type a path directly (a Unix-style "/like/this/" is tidied up
automatically), or press that field's own "Select..." button to
connect and browse into the share first. Then press Save - BackupVault
builds a Mountlist entry on the fly in DEVS:DOSDrivers/ and runs the
standard AmigaDOS Mount command against it, then confirms the resulting
volume is actually accessible before accepting it as a Repository - a
wrong password or an unreachable server is refused with an explanation
rather than silently saved as broken.

Volume name is not a separate field - the Repository's own Name doubles
as the AmigaDOS volume it's mounted as (characters a volume name can't
use are replaced with '-').

The Mountlist file BackupVault writes to DEVS:DOSDrivers/ is left in
place once mounting succeeds, so the share stays mounted after a reboot
too, without reopening BackupVault - it's only removed again if you
delete the Repository from within BackupVault (which also unmounts the
volume). This does mean deleting a Repository is the correct way to
retire an SMB/SFTP share, not just deleting it from the list and
forgetting about it.

SMB Repositories, mounted by BackupVault itself exactly as described
above, have been confirmed working under WinUAE - no WinUAE-specific
handling is needed anywhere in this program.

Once mounted, an SMB/SFTP Repository works exactly like a Local one
everywhere else in the program - disk space is shown for it in the
Repository manager's list the same way. The File System column shows
"smb2-handler"/"ssh2-handler" plus that handler's own version (read
from its $VER: string in L:) instead of a real filesystem type, since
smb2fs/ssh2fs don't report one through the same DOS call a local
filesystem does. For a Local Repository, this is identified by asking
the actual handler task managing that volume, not just by reading its
reported DOS type - a volume can be set up to report a plain FFS-style
type while a different handler (most commonly PFS3) is really running
it, which the DOS type alone can't reveal.

The Repository manager (Backup tab's "Repository" button, or Menu/
Manage/Repositories...) lists every Repository with its Name, Type,
Path, a Size column showing the destination's total and free space
(e.g. "20 GB (14 GB free)"), and the File System column described above
(e.g. "PFS3 19.4" for a Local Repository, "smb2-handler 3.2" for an SMB
one).

A Local Repository's row also shows a @{b}Max Filename@{ub} column - the
longest filename that volume can actually store, found by actually
creating and immediately deleting a test file of various lengths on it.
This is always tested for real rather than assumed from the volume's
reported filesystem type, even for what looks like plain FFS - as just
explained above, the DOS type alone can't reveal whether a different
handler with a different real limit is actually managing it. Shown as
"-" for SMB/SFTP Repositories, since the real limit there is whatever
the remote server enforces. This matters most for a Sync Job's
destination - see
@{" Sync Jobs " LINK SYNCJOBS} for what happens when a file's name is
too long for it.

New/Edit work the same way as for a job: pick one from the list (or
double-click it), then Edit, or use New to define one from scratch.
Delete refuses (listing which job(s) are affected) if any Backup Job
still writes to that Repository - point those jobs at a different
Repository first.

Go back to the @{" main menu " LINK MAIN}.
@endnode


@node CREDENTIALS "Credentials"
@{b}Credentials@{ub}

A Credential is a saved username/password/note, managed from Menu /
Manage / Credentials:

  @{b}Add Credential@{ub}    - opens a blank Username/Password/Note form.
  @{b}Edit Credential@{ub}   - opens the selected Credential's form,
                    pre-filled (including its password), or
                    double-click a row in the list.
  @{b}Delete Credential@{ub} - removes the selected Credential (after
                    confirming). Does not affect any Repository already
                    saved using it - its username/password were copied
                    in at Save time (see @{" Repositories " LINK
                    REPOSITORIES}), so there's no live link to break;
                    it just won't be offered as a choice any more.

The list shows each Credential's Username, Password (always shown as
**** regardless of its actual length or content - the list is for
telling entries apart, not for reading passwords back), Note, and the
date it was created.

SMB Share and SFTP Repositories pick their Username/Password from a
Credential (a Cycle button in the Repository dialog, showing
"username (note)" with the note shortened to 12 characters) instead of
typing them in per Repository, so the same server account can be
reused without retyping it and only needs updating in one place if the
password ever changes. If you don't have a Credential yet, the
Repository dialog's own "Add Credential" button opens the same Add
Credential window without losing what you were doing there.

@{b}Passwords are stored in plain text@{ub} in
PROGDIR:data/BackupVault.credentials, same as everything else this
program saves - classic AmigaOS has no OS-level credential vault.

Go back to the @{" main menu " LINK MAIN}.
@endnode


@node CREATEJOB "Creating a Backup Job"
@{b}Creating a Backup Job@{ub}

From the Backup tab's "Create Backup" button (Type cycle set to "File
Backup"):

  @{b}Name@{ub}       - must be unique among your jobs.
  @{b}Source@{ub}     - a directory, or a whole disk/volume. The Select...
              button opens directly at the volume list (ASL's
              "show volumes" mode), so picking a whole disk as the
              source is one click - DoBackup() treats a volume root
              exactly like any other directory.
  @{b}Repository@{ub} - which saved Repository to write the archive into -
              see @{" Repositories " LINK REPOSITORIES}.

  @{b}Backup file suffix@{ub}:
    - Add Time suffix - a timestamp per run, so repeated runs don't
      overwrite each other.
    - None - always the same archive name; overwriting an existing one
      asks for confirmation first.
    - Custom suffix - your own fixed text appended to the job name.

  @{b}Compression@{ub} - None, LZSS, or Deflate (zlib) - see
              @{" Compression " LINK COMPRESSION}.
  @{b}Exclude patterns@{ub} (optional) - a semicolon-separated list of
              AmigaDOS wildcard patterns, e.g. "#?.tmp;T;Cache", matched
              against each item's own bare name (not its full path),
              anywhere in the source tree at any depth. A matching
              directory is skipped entirely, along with everything
              under it. The job's log lists every excluded item by name
              ("Excluded: ...") and the final summary shows how many
              were left out.
  @{b}Check archive after backup@{ub} - runs a full Check immediately after
              a successful backup, same as pressing Check by hand
              afterwards - see @{" Check " LINK CHECKARCHIVE}.
  @{b}Scan for viruses before backup (xvs.library)@{ub} - if the optional
              third-party xvs.library is installed, each file is read
              into memory and checked before being archived. The job's
              log shows the result right on that file's own line, e.g.
              "File : UnZipSFX (76836 bytes) (scanned : passed)" - or
              "(scanned : VIRUS WARNING - <name>)", or
              "(not scanned : <reason>)" for the rare file that
              couldn't be checked (too large, a read error). A flagged
              file is still backed up as normal (an archived virus
              can't do anything until something extracts and runs it) -
              this only adds that log text and a summary in the job's
              final result message, it never blocks or skips the file.
              Off by default, and does nothing at all if xvs.library
              isn't installed (logged once, at the top of
              the log, rather than for every file). Since the whole
              file has to be read an extra time to be scanned, expect a
              backup with this on to take roughly twice as long.
  @{b}Retention (keep last N, 0 = keep all)@{ub} - after each successful
              run, older archives from this same job beyond the N most
              recent are deleted automatically. 0 (the default) keeps
              every archive forever.
  @{b}Note@{ub}       - optional free text, stored in the archive itself and
              shown in the History tab.
  @{b}Schedule@{ub}   - see @{" Scheduling " LINK SCHEDULING}.

Double-clicking a job in the list opens it for editing, same as the
Edit Job button. A job's kind (File Backup or Sync Job) cannot be
changed while editing - delete and recreate it instead if you need to.

Pressing Start on a Backup Job first estimates the source's total size
and compares it against the destination's free space; if it looks like
there may not be enough room, you get a warning with both numbers and a
chance to cancel before anything is written. This check only runs for a
manual Start press, not a scheduled/unattended run, so it never adds a
full extra directory scan to every automated backup.

The Start button at the bottom of the window runs whichever job is
selected in the list - see @{" Running a job " LINK RUNNINGJOBS}.

Go back to the @{" main menu " LINK MAIN}.
@endnode


@node SYNCJOBS "Sync Jobs"
@{b}Sync Jobs@{ub}

A Sync Job keeps two directories (Path A and Path B) aligned with each
other, independent of the Backup Job/archive system entirely - no .bku
file is produced. Created the same way as a Backup Job, with the
Create Job dialog's own Type cycle set to "Sync Job". Two modes:

  @{b}Two-way@{ub}            - each run makes both directories match,
                   copying whatever is new/changed on either side to
                   the other, and deleting on either side whatever was
                   deleted on the other since the last run.
  @{b}One-way (A -> B)@{ub}   - copies changes from A to B only, and
                   deletes from B whatever was deleted from A; changes
                   made directly in B are never pushed back to A. If B
                   is missing a file it should already have (deleted by
                   hand, or B points at storage that doesn't persist
                   between runs, such as a RAM disk cleared on reboot),
                   it is copied again even if A itself hasn't changed -
                   one-way sync always makes sure B actually has what A
                   says it should, rather than just trusting last
                   time's result.

Sync Jobs use a saved baseline (PROGDIR:data/BackupVault.sync.<name> -
see @{" Files this program uses " LINK FILESCREATED}) of what both sides
looked like after the last successful run, and MD5 checksums to tell a
real content change from a file that was merely touched, to decide
what actually needs copying or deleting.

@{b}Sync Job is new and still under active development and long-term
testing@{ub} - saving your first one shows a one-time warning to that
effect and to keep an independent backup of both directories before
relying on it, since a bug here could copy or delete files
unexpectedly; report any problem you find. Running a Sync Job by hand
asks for confirmation each time with three choices: @{b}Continue@{ub}
(run it for real), @{b}Preview@{ub} (see exactly what it would
copy/delete/flag as a conflict, logged with "Would..." lines to its own
<name>.sync-preview.log and opened immediately in the log viewer,
without touching either directory or the saved baseline at all - safe
to run as many times as you like), or @{b}Cancel@{ub}. A scheduled
(unattended) run skips this prompt entirely and always runs for real,
since there's no one there to answer it - see
@{" Scheduling " LINK SCHEDULING}.

Preview cannot warn about a destination that silently rejects a
specific file (e.g. a filesystem with a shorter maximum filename than
the source allows - see @{" Repositories " LINK REPOSITORIES}'s Max
Filename column) since it never actually touches the destination; a
real Continue run logs that plainly instead ("FAILED to copy ...") if
it happens, and keeps retrying that file on every future run. Fixing it
means either reformatting the destination with long-filename support or
renaming the file, not something Backup Vault can work around on its
own.

Go back to the @{" main menu " LINK MAIN}.
@endnode


@node COMPRESSION "Compression"
@{b}Compression@{ub}

Each Backup Job independently chooses one of:

  @{b}None@{ub}    - files are stored as-is.
  @{b}LZSS@{ub}    - a small, dependency-free compressor built into the
             program itself.
  @{b}Deflate@{ub} - real zlib compression, via the third-party z.library
             (see @{" Requirements " LINK REQUIREMENTS}). If z.library is
             not installed when a Deflate job actually runs, its files
             are simply stored uncompressed instead - nothing fails,
             but check the Restore tab's Compression column to see
             what a given archive actually ended up using.

Go back to the @{" main menu " LINK MAIN}.
@endnode


@node SCHEDULING "Scheduling"
@{b}Scheduling@{ub}

Any Backup or Sync Job can be given a Schedule (in its Create/Edit
dialog): Off (the default - manual only), Daily/Weekly/Monthly at a
chosen time (Weekly also picks a day of the week, Monthly a day of the
month 1-31), or every 15/30 minutes or 1/3/6/12 hours.

A Daily/Weekly/Monthly job catches up once if BackupVault is opened
after today's/this week's/this month's scheduled time has already
passed and it hasn't run yet in that period - it does not catch up for
periods it was closed for entirely. An Interval job is purely
elapsed-time based: if overdue when the program starts, it runs once
soon after, then resumes its normal cadence from there. Either way,
BackupVault checks for due jobs about every 30 seconds while it is
running.

Classic AmigaOS has no background-service mechanism, so a schedule only
takes effect while BackupVault's own process is actually running - a
scheduled job never runs while the program is not open. A scheduled
Sync Job also skips its usual confirmation prompt when it fires on its
own (there is no one there to answer it) - only a manual Start press
still asks first.

To have BackupVault start automatically at boot and sit quietly in the
background (so its schedules can actually run unattended):
  1. Put BackupVault (or an icon that runs it) in your WBStartup:
     drawer.
  2. Open that icon's Information window (Workbench's Icons menu) and
     add a Tool Type: STARTICONIFIED
Launched this way, BackupVault opens iconified (an icon on Workbench,
no visible window) instead of popping up its window - double-click
that icon at any time to open the window normally. A normal double-
click launch (no STARTICONIFIED, or launched from a Shell) always opens
the window as usual.

See @{" Headless daemon " LINK DAEMON} for BackupVaultDaemon, an
alternative, still-experimental way to run scheduled jobs without
BackupVault's own window open at all.

Go back to the @{" main menu " LINK MAIN}.
@endnode


@node DAEMON "Headless daemon"
@{b}BackupVaultDaemon@{ub}

@{b}EXPERIMENTAL - STILL IN DEVELOPMENT.@{ub} BackupVaultDaemon is a
separate, tiny CLI tool (no MUI, no window at all) that runs your
saved, scheduled Backup/Sync Jobs headlessly - an alternative to
keeping BackupVault itself open/iconified just for scheduling.

  Run >NIL: BackupVaultDaemon
runs it continuously (suitable for S:User-Startup); or run

  BackupVaultDaemon ONESHOT
from a Shell to check once for due jobs and exit (useful for testing).

Do NOT run this at the same time as leaving BackupVault's own window
open with jobs scheduled - the two do not coordinate with each other,
so the same job could fire from both at once. Pick one scheduler for
your jobs, not both.

This tool has only been verified to compile - it has NOT been proven
through extended real-world testing on real or emulated Amiga hardware
yet. Before relying on it, please test it yourself with ONESHOT first,
then watch it run unattended over a real scheduled interval, on a
Repository/directories you can afford to lose, with an independent
backup kept elsewhere regardless. If you find a problem, please report
it - see @{" Contact & credits " LINK CONTACT}.

Go back to the @{" main menu " LINK MAIN}.
@endnode


@node WEBDAEMON "Web daemon"
@{b}BackupVaultWebDaemon@{ub}

@{b}EXPERIMENTAL - STILL IN DEVELOPMENT - NOT YET INTEGRATED INTO THE
GUI.@{ub} BackupVaultWebDaemon is a separate, standalone HTTP server that
lets you browse and download the contents of your saved Repositories
from an ordinary web browser on the same network, without opening
BackupVault itself.

Run it from a Shell:

  BackupVaultWebDaemon
uses the default port, 1453. Override it with:

  BackupVaultWebDaemon PORT=8080

Point a browser at the Amiga's IP address and that port to see a list
of Repositories, browse into them, and download individual files.

This is a first working prototype, not a finished feature:

  - It serves plain HTTP with no authentication or encryption - anyone
    who can reach that port on your network can browse and download
    from every Repository it lists. Treat it as a convenience for a
    small, trusted local network, not something to expose to the
    internet or an untrusted network.
  - It is read-only (no upload, delete, or partial-range support) and
    handles one request at a time.
  - There is currently no button in the BackupVault GUI to start or
    stop it - it has to be run separately, by hand or from your own
    startup script.
  - It has been verified to compile and run, but has not had anywhere
    near the real-world use the rest of the program has. Its command-
    line options and behavior may change before it's considered
    stable, and it may eventually be integrated into the main GUI
    (started/stopped from a button there) if it proves out.

If you try it, please report anything unexpected - see
@{" Contact & credits " LINK CONTACT}.

Go back to the @{" main menu " LINK MAIN}.
@endnode


@node RUNNINGJOBS "Running a job"
@{b}Running a job@{ub}

Select a job in the Backup tab's list and press Start. The gauge and
status line below it track progress and show the exact result once the
run finishes (e.g. "Backup completed successfully."). Pause suspends a
running job (Resume continues it); Stop cancels it - for a Backup Job,
the incomplete archive is deleted and the cancellation is logged,
rather than leaving a partial, unusable file behind.

If files are added to or removed from the source while a backup is
mid-run, BackupVault does not (and cannot, on classic AmigaOS) freeze a
perfectly consistent snapshot of it - there is no OS-level mechanism
for that, the way VSS/LVM/ZFS snapshots work elsewhere. A file removed
after being listed but before being read is logged as a failure for
that one file only, without corrupting the rest of the archive; a file
added mid-run is usually picked up too, just with a harmless note in
the log if the final count doesn't match the original scan. For
anything you care about being consistent down to the second, avoid
actively changing it while its backup is running.

Go back to the @{" main menu " LINK MAIN}.
@endnode


@node RESTORETAB "The Restore tab"
@{b}The Restore tab@{ub}

The Restore tab shows a tree built straight from the filesystem - one
parent node per Backup Job, one leaf per archive found in that job's
Repository. Selecting a leaf enables three buttons:

  @{" Full Restore "   LINK FULLRESTORE}
  @{" Restore Files "  LINK RESTOREFILES}
  @{" Check "          LINK CHECKARCHIVE}

@{" Import "  LINK IMPORTBACKUP} (on the Backup tab) adds an archive that
wasn't created by a BackupVault Job into this tree.

Go back to the @{" main menu " LINK MAIN}.
@endnode


@node FULLRESTORE "Full Restore"
@{b}Full Restore@{ub}

Restores the entire selected archive into a directory you choose. Opens
its own small window pre-filled with the archive and a destination
field (with its own Select... browser); its own Start button closes
that window and runs the restore through the main window's shared
progress gauge.

Go back to the @{" main menu " LINK MAIN}.
@endnode


@node RESTOREFILES "Restore Files"
@{b}Restore Files@{ub}

Opens the selected archive's file list so you can check exactly which
files/folders to restore, rather than the whole thing - checking a
folder includes everything under it. Its own "Restore Selected" button
closes the window and runs a filtered restore through the same shared
progress gauge Full Restore uses.

Go back to the @{" main menu " LINK MAIN}.
@endnode


@node CHECKARCHIVE "Check"
@{b}Check@{ub}

Verifies the selected archive's integrity - recomputing and comparing
every entry's stored checksum - without restoring anything, without
its own window; it runs immediately through the shared progress gauge
and reports success/failure the same way a backup or restore does.

Go back to the @{" main menu " LINK MAIN}.
@endnode


@node IMPORTBACKUP "Import"
@{b}Import@{ub}

Lets you pick a .bku archive that was not created by a BackupVault Job
- from the Backup tab's "Import" button. BackupVault reads that
archive's own saved metadata (job name, source path, Repository name/
path, note, compression type) and automatically creates a matching
Repository and/or Backup Job for it if they don't already exist, then
shows it in the Restore tree like any other archive.

Go back to the @{" main menu " LINK MAIN}.
@endnode


@node HISTORYTAB "The History tab"
@{b}The History tab@{ub}

Every backup/restore/sync/check/deletion appears as a row (Type, Name,
Date, Size, Location), searchable via the tab's own search field.
Buttons:

  @{b}Search@{ub}    - filters the list to rows matching the typed text.
  @{b}Refresh@{ub}   - rebuilds the list from the catalog on disk.
  @{b}View Log@{ub}  - shows the selected run's own full log file (or
              double-click the row) in its own window - one line per
              row, with anything worth noticing (a warning, a virus-
              scan hit, a failure/cancellation) shown in bold. That
              window's own "Open in Multiview" button opens the same
              file in Multiview instead, for its full-text search or
              printing.

The History menu adds two distinct, non-overlapping actions:

  @{b}Remove All Logs@{ub} - deletes only the per-run log files under
                  PROGDIR:logs. The History list itself is untouched -
                  View Log simply finds nothing for those older rows
                  afterward.
  @{b}Remove History@{ub}  - clears the History list (the catalog) itself
                  instead - a separate, more drastic action. Log files
                  on disk are not touched by this one.

Go back to the @{" main menu " LINK MAIN}.
@endnode


@node CLITOOL "The command-line tool"
@{b}The command-line tool (Backup)@{ub}

A separate command-line program, sharing the same archive format and
doing the actual work behind every BackupVault GUI operation - for
scripted use from a Shell without the GUI, or from AmigaDOS scripts/
Jobs, Repositories, or Sync.

  Backup SOURCE DEST
      SOURCE (a disk or directory) is backed up into archive DEST.
  Backup SOURCE DEST RESTORE
      SOURCE (an archive file) is restored into directory DEST.
  Backup SOURCE LIST
      Lists the contents of archive file SOURCE.

A backup or restore run also writes its own
"<name>.backup.log"/"<name>.restore.log" file under PROGDIR:logs, the
same as a job run from the GUI does.

Go back to the @{" main menu " LINK MAIN}.
@endnode


@node FILESCREATED "Files this program uses"
@{b}Files created by the program@{ub}

  PROGDIR:logs/                         - one log file per backup/
                                          restore/sync/check/Sync
                                          Preview run.
  PROGDIR:data/BackupVault.catalog      - the History tab's data: every
                                          past backup/restore/sync/
                                          check/deletion.
  PROGDIR:data/BackupVault.repos        - saved Repositories, including
                                          SMB/SFTP connection details
                                          and passwords in plain text -
                                          see @{" Repositories " LINK REPOSITORIES}.
  PROGDIR:data/BackupVault.credentials  - saved Credentials, in plain
                                          text - see @{" Credentials "
                                          LINK CREDENTIALS}.
  PROGDIR:data/BackupVault.jobs         - saved Backup Jobs.
  PROGDIR:data/BackupVault.jobextras    - a Backup Job's "Scan for
                                          viruses before backup" setting
                                          and Exclude patterns - see
                                          @{" Creating a Backup Job "
                                          LINK CREATEJOB} - kept separate
                                          from BackupVault.jobs, as plain
                                          text rather than a fixed-size
                                          binary record, so it can keep
                                          gaining fields like this again
                                          later without any risk to
                                          already-saved jobs.
  PROGDIR:data/BackupVault.syncjobs     - saved Sync Jobs.
  PROGDIR:data/BackupVault.sync.<name>  - one per Sync Job, its
                                          baseline (what both sides
                                          looked like after its last
                                          successful run - see
                                          @{" Sync Jobs " LINK SYNCJOBS}).
  DEVS:DOSDrivers/<volume>              - one per mounted SMB/SFTP
                                          Repository, kept until that
                                          Repository is deleted - see
                                          @{" Repositories " LINK REPOSITORIES}.

Everything under PROGDIR:data/ used to sit directly under PROGDIR:
instead (as plain PROGDIR:BackupVault.repos etc.) before version 0.6.4
- an existing install's files are moved into PROGDIR:data/
automatically, once, the first time you run this version.

None of these are required for the program to run, and can be deleted
freely - you will simply lose your History list and your saved
Repositories/Credentials/Jobs (and, for a Sync Job's own baseline file,
its next run will treat every file as new rather than picking up where
the last run left off).

Go back to the @{" main menu " LINK MAIN}.
@endnode


@node LIMITATIONS "Known limitations"
@{b}Known limitations@{ub}

  - Every backup produces a full, standalone archive - there is no
    incremental backup support.
  - The built-in LZSS compression is a small, from-scratch
    implementation, chosen to avoid extra library dependencies. It
    will not compress as well as Deflate or a dedicated compressor.
  - Sync Jobs are new and still being tested in real-world use - see
    @{" Sync Jobs " LINK SYNCJOBS} and @{" Disclaimer " LINK DISCLAIMER}.
  - BackupVaultDaemon and BackupVaultWebDaemon are both still under
    active development - see @{" Headless daemon " LINK DAEMON} and
    @{" Web daemon " LINK WEBDAEMON}. Do not rely on either as your
    only means of running scheduled jobs, and do not expose
    BackupVaultWebDaemon beyond a trusted local network.
  - There is no snapshot/point-in-time consistency mechanism - classic
    AmigaOS doesn't provide one a userland program could use - see
    @{" Running a job " LINK RUNNINGJOBS}.
  - SMB/SFTP Credential passwords, and a Repository's own copy of them,
    are stored in plain text - see
    @{" Files this program uses " LINK FILESCREATED}.
  - As beta-quality software generally, testing so far has been
    limited - see @{" Disclaimer " LINK DISCLAIMER}.

Go back to the @{" main menu " LINK MAIN}.
@endnode


@node DISCLAIMER "Disclaimer"
@{b}This is beta-quality software - please read this@{ub}

BackupVault reads from and writes to your filesystem, including
restoring and syncing files that can overwrite or delete existing
ones. Like any software, it may contain bugs that could lead to data
loss or other damage. Sync Jobs, BackupVaultDaemon, and
BackupVaultWebDaemon (see @{" Sync Jobs " LINK SYNCJOBS},
@{" Headless daemon " LINK DAEMON}, @{" Web daemon " LINK WEBDAEMON}) in
particular are new and still under active development.

By downloading, installing, or running this software, you agree that:

  - You do so entirely at your own risk, of your own free will, as a
    voluntary test of beta software.
  - You keep an independent backup of anything you are not prepared to
    lose, made by some other means, before relying on this program for
    it.
  - The developer accepts no responsibility or liability for any data
    loss, corruption, or other damage resulting from the use of this
    software.

If that is not acceptable to you, please do not use this program.

Go back to the @{" main menu " LINK MAIN}.
@endnode


@node CONTACT "Contact & credits"
@{b}Contact@{ub}

Serkan Dursun
blasterreal@{"@"}gmail.com

Please include the relevant log file from PROGDIR:logs/ when reporting
a problem, if you can.

@{b}Credits@{ub}

Built with SAS/C 6.58 and MUI, using the third-party TabGroup.mcc and
NList.mcc/NListview.mcc/NListtree.mcc custom classes. Deflate
compression uses the third-party z.library (salass00/z_lib). MD5 (used
by Sync Jobs to detect real content changes) is Colin Plumb's 1993
public-domain implementation. SMB Share and SFTP Repositories are
backed by the third-party smb2fs and ssh2-handler by Fredrik Wikstrom
(salass00/smb2fs, ssh2-handler) - neither is included with this
program and must be installed separately if you want to use them.
Virus scanning uses the third-party xvs.library by Georg Wittmann and
the Virus Help Team - also not included, install it separately if you
want to use that option.

Go back to the @{" main menu " LINK MAIN}.
@endnode
